{
  "chapter": "reliability/idempotency-in-practice",
  "tier": "build",
  "notes": [
    "Exactly-once delivery does not exist. Exactly-once effect does: at-least-once delivery",
    "plus a deduplication store you own.",
    "The record must be more durable than whatever would repeat the call, which rules out the",
    "agent's own state \u2014 replaying that state is the thing being defended against.",
    "Three states, not two. The IN_FLIGHT row is what makes a concurrent duplicate wait rather",
    "than double-execute, and its lease must outlast the slowest legitimate call.",
    "Commit the effect and the record in one transaction. A marker in Redis and a write in",
    "Postgres reintroduces the crash gap; when the effect is external, use an outbox.",
    "The window is the bug. It must exceed the longest interval over which the same operation",
    "could be attempted, which for agents is the human approval pause rather than the retry",
    "policy \u2014 a run that pauses Friday and resumes Monday is outside a 24-hour convention.",
    "A FAILED row is retryable only when the failure was provably before the effect. A timeout",
    "is not, so it escalates rather than retrying.",
    "Alert when a key expires while its run is still alive."
  ],
  "config": {
    "leaseMs": 60000,
    "approvalPauseMs": 259200000
  },
  "cases": [
